WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.375 --> 00:00:01.751
“Sinus suffering.”

00:00:01.751 --> 00:00:04.754
On the left is a decongestant commercial 
from 2000.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.507
On the right one from 2006.

00:00:07.632 --> 00:00:10.677
But don't focus on the pressure pads 
or exploding heads.

00:00:11.052 --> 00:00:12.220
Look at the boxes.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.556
Sudafed and Sudafed PE.

00:00:14.556 --> 00:00:15.640
PE. 

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.184
Sudafed usually uses pseudoephedrine.

00:00:18.226 --> 00:00:21.563
Sudafed PE stands for phenylephrine.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:25.025
Today, phenylephrine vastly outsells 
pseudoephedrine.

00:00:25.316 --> 00:00:27.694
Yet, I am still congested.

00:00:28.236 --> 00:00:31.156
I was popping one of these daily 
in the morning, every morning...

00:00:31.489 --> 00:00:32.574
and it just didn't work.

00:00:32.574 --> 00:00:35.535
But it's not me and it's not you.

00:00:36.119 --> 00:00:37.078
That's because...

00:00:37.078 --> 00:00:39.456
“This drug is really not effective.”

00:00:39.456 --> 00:00:43.293
“10 milligrams of phenylephrine 
works no better than a placebo.”

00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:46.212
“Now, there are serious questions
about whether it works at all.”

00:00:46.296 --> 00:00:48.339
Taken from an FDA Zoom meeting

00:00:48.339 --> 00:00:50.967
these are the votes of FDA panel members

00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:53.094
on whether phenylephrine is effective.

00:00:53.178 --> 00:00:54.220
“Yeah, I voted no.”

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.263
“I voted no.”

00:00:55.263 --> 00:00:56.806
“-I voted no.”
“-I voted no.”

00:00:56.806 --> 00:00:58.224
“The data are pretty clear.”

00:00:58.224 --> 00:00:59.559
How did this happen?

00:00:59.851 --> 00:01:01.853
It is a story that goes back decades

00:01:01.853 --> 00:01:05.148
and touches on methamphetamine, 
COVID...

00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:06.983
and most importantly

00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:10.320
why you were able to buy 
a decongestant... 

00:01:10.779 --> 00:01:11.613
that didn't work.

00:01:11.863 --> 00:01:14.824
[dramatic cello music]

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.285
Imagine a grocery store.

00:01:17.702 --> 00:01:19.996
The FDA regulates food over here...

00:01:20.205 --> 00:01:22.332
and the prescription drugs over here.

00:01:22.373 --> 00:01:24.501
These are both massive jobs.

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:27.087
And a separate FDA job is here:

00:01:27.337 --> 00:01:30.006
The over-the-counter drugs: OTC.

00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:33.301
Available for anyone to just buy 
and walk out.

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:36.346
And that is what I have been buying.

00:01:36.471 --> 00:01:39.224
[curious piano music]

00:01:39.224 --> 00:01:41.351
In 1962, the federal government

00:01:41.351 --> 00:01:43.978
charged the FDA with a new responsibility:

00:01:44.187 --> 00:01:46.523
Instead of just regulating the sale of drugs...

00:01:46.731 --> 00:01:47.941
they had to start reviewing

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:50.360
the safety and effectiveness of them.

00:01:50.568 --> 00:01:53.029
They started here, in the prescription section...

00:01:53.321 --> 00:01:54.948
because they were more worried about it.

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:58.326
Ten years later, they got to 
the OTC drugs.

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:00.870
This proposal outlined the plan.

00:02:01.704 --> 00:02:03.331
But this shelf was big.

00:02:03.665 --> 00:02:06.876
100,000 to 500,000 products, big.

00:02:07.335 --> 00:02:09.546
And it was too much to thoroughly 
work through.

00:02:10.046 --> 00:02:12.507
Well, I think people perceive...

00:02:12.841 --> 00:02:14.676
that because something sold 
over the counter...

00:02:15.343 --> 00:02:19.055
it has been reviewed more rigorously,
but that is just not true.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:23.893
A lot of the products and the research 
dates back decades.

00:02:24.227 --> 00:02:25.520
That's Joshua Sharfstein.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.730
He is a professor, but also a former...

00:02:27.939 --> 00:02:30.525
principal deputy commissioner at the FDA.

00:02:30.775 --> 00:02:33.570
He helped take over-the-counter
cough and cold medicines

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:36.573
off the market for kids 
back in the early 2000s.

00:02:36.781 --> 00:02:38.408
But in the 70s and 80s

00:02:38.408 --> 00:02:41.661
the FDA said that drug ingredients 
already on the shelf...

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:44.873
could stay if they were generally recognized

00:02:44.873 --> 00:02:47.250
as safe and effective by medical professionals.

00:02:47.292 --> 00:02:50.503
By 1976, they'd convened a looser review

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:52.755
and a panel recommended Phenylephrine

00:02:52.755 --> 00:02:55.300
as an oral, nasal decongestant.

00:02:55.383 --> 00:02:59.637
They also recommended phenylpropanolamine
and pseudoephedrine.

00:02:59.888 --> 00:03:01.514
We’ll make phenylephrine red...

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:04.100
because we know it leaves you congested.

00:03:05.643 --> 00:03:06.853
Like me. 

00:03:07.395 --> 00:03:10.190
Pseudoephedrine and blue, 
because it does work.

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:12.275
And phenylpropanolamine grey.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:13.860
I'll tell you why later.

00:03:14.444 --> 00:03:18.406
This list of nasal decongestants
still mostly grandfathered in...

00:03:18.615 --> 00:03:21.242
was further codified by the 1990s.

00:03:21.659 --> 00:03:25.121
So you had options on the shelf, 
sold under many names...

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:29.334
and pseudoephedrine became 
the OTC nose-unclogger of choice

00:03:29.334 --> 00:03:30.752
through the 80s and 90s. 

00:03:31.127 --> 00:03:34.964
And that was kind of the status
quo with commercials like these:

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:37.050
“Feel well enough to get married, honey?”

00:03:37.342 --> 00:03:38.092
“I do.”

00:03:38.301 --> 00:03:41.429
“Fast-acting Sudafed and long-lasting 
Sudafed 12 hour.”

00:03:41.429 --> 00:03:44.182
“Take non-drowsy Sudafed at your first sign.”

00:03:44.432 --> 00:03:45.808
“Fight back with Sudafed.”

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:47.727
But then... this happened.

00:03:47.936 --> 00:03:50.563
“Pseudoephedrine, a common ingredient

00:03:50.563 --> 00:03:52.106
in cold medicines like Sudafed

00:03:52.106 --> 00:03:54.943
is an essential part of 
making methamphetamine.”

00:03:55.151 --> 00:03:57.946
“In response to that, Congress in 2005

00:03:57.946 --> 00:04:00.406
enacts the Combat Methamphetamine Act.”

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:03.868
If you need it clearer, imagine Walt and Jesse
from Breaking Bad

00:04:03.868 --> 00:04:06.246
crushing up pseudoephedrine to make meth.

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:08.581
This law, tacked on to the Patriot Act...

00:04:08.831 --> 00:04:11.376
largely meant pseudoephedrine
decongestants

00:04:11.376 --> 00:04:13.211
were put behind the counter.

00:04:13.211 --> 00:04:15.630
In many states, you could buy it 
without a prescription...

00:04:15.964 --> 00:04:18.633
but you had to be ID'd by a pharmacist...

00:04:18.883 --> 00:04:21.344
and were limited in how much you could get.

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:24.472
“I mean, it's getting harder 
and harder to come by.”

00:04:24.681 --> 00:04:28.935
Phenylpropanolamine was generally considered 
unsafe by 2000...

00:04:29.185 --> 00:04:31.479
because it caused some hemorrhagic strokes.

00:04:31.813 --> 00:04:34.232
Phenylaphrine was alone on the shelf.

00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:37.902
Meth-finding officials asked
companies like Pfizer:

00:04:37.902 --> 00:04:39.112
“What took so long?”

00:04:39.195 --> 00:04:41.072
But the FDA's long process meant

00:04:41.072 --> 00:04:43.324
they couldn't just spin up a new product.

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:45.618
Commercials started to look like this:

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:48.037
“The new Sudafed PE
Triple Action.”

00:04:48.663 --> 00:04:50.498
“Also find Sudafed behind the counter.”

00:04:50.665 --> 00:04:52.125
But even as manufacturers

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:54.794
tried to straddle the lines, 
phenylaphrine sales...

00:04:54.919 --> 00:04:57.547
this line, started to beat out this line:

00:04:57.672 --> 00:04:59.632
pseudoephedrine decongestants.

00:04:59.674 --> 00:05:02.468
Basically because it was alone on the shelf.

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:06.097
Now, you probably saw this recent story 
about the FDA panel

00:05:06.097 --> 00:05:08.141
recommending against phenylephrine

00:05:08.141 --> 00:05:11.144
and assumed that they just figured out 
that it didn't work.

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:12.895
That is not the case.

00:05:13.229 --> 00:05:14.772
They have known for decades

00:05:14.772 --> 00:05:17.442
that phenylephrine might not really work...

00:05:17.942 --> 00:05:20.611
and it's just the process 
that's gotten in the way.

00:05:22.238 --> 00:05:24.490
“Sudafed’s replacement no winner”

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.992
was a 2006 headline as the key.

00:05:26.075 --> 00:05:30.079
As the key researchers behind that report 
recalled on the panel this year...

00:05:30.246 --> 00:05:32.623
After pseudoephedrine was removed

00:05:32.623 --> 00:05:34.459
in front of the counter 
to behind the counter...

00:05:34.792 --> 00:05:38.046
I received a rash of calls from 
around the state of Florida...

00:05:38.421 --> 00:05:41.883
asking why oral Phenylephrine didn't work.

00:05:42.008 --> 00:05:45.470
The take home messages are oral 
phenylephrine is inaffective

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:47.472
as a nasal decongestant but safe.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.850
They found that most of the studies
ruling it effective

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:51.851
came from one lab:

00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:53.269
Elizabeth Biochemical.

00:05:53.311 --> 00:05:56.522
The researchers letter to the FDA from 2006

00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:58.274
attracted congressional support...

00:05:58.566 --> 00:06:00.276
from Representative Henry Waxman.

00:06:00.401 --> 00:06:03.446
It led to further similarly conclusive studies...

00:06:03.654 --> 00:06:05.573
that phenylephrine was ineffective.

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:09.494
So why has it stayed on the shelf 
for almost two more decades?

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:11.788
“We'll hear from Dr. Janet Woodcock

00:06:11.788 --> 00:06:15.666
director of the FDA's Center for 
Drug Evaluation and Research.”

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:18.669
Dr. Janet Woodcock shared this list of burdensome...

00:06:18.878 --> 00:06:21.422
multi-step rule makings that were typical

00:06:21.422 --> 00:06:24.258
for new drugs or revisions to old ones.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:27.720
“This process is frozen in 1972 and before...

00:06:28.179 --> 00:06:31.307
so it doesn't apply to anything later than that.”

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:34.977
“So, this is only still trying to deal
with those products

00:06:34.977 --> 00:06:36.646
that were on the market at that time.”

00:06:36.938 --> 00:06:41.526
“So, there's really nothing for innovation
in this entire process.”

00:06:41.818 --> 00:06:44.404
That matched with significantly smaller
funding for

00:06:44.404 --> 00:06:48.199
the FDA's over-the-counter division 
versus the prescription drug one.

00:06:48.241 --> 00:06:51.953
It took until the 2020 CARES Act, 
a Coronavirus relief bill...

00:06:52.328 --> 00:06:54.872
to make the drug slightly easier to amend...

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:58.334
and to give the FDA's over-the-counter division 
a bit more funding.

00:06:58.376 --> 00:07:00.128
The FDA had its hands...

00:07:00.336 --> 00:07:01.963
tied behind its back when thinking

00:07:01.963 --> 00:07:05.758
about over-the-counter medicines, 
and now it has more tools

00:07:05.758 --> 00:07:08.219
and more resources to go about its job.

00:07:08.219 --> 00:07:11.139
And in 2023, the advisory panel

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:15.017
finally reviewed new studies 
filled with charts like these.

00:07:15.268 --> 00:07:18.938
Where nasal congestion went down 
after taking pseudoephedrine...

00:07:19.313 --> 00:07:21.899
and went like this for a placebo...

00:07:22.525 --> 00:07:23.734
and phenylephrine.

00:07:24.277 --> 00:07:25.486
Now we wait.

00:07:25.987 --> 00:07:27.280
It is true that all this time

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:28.698
I could have gone behind the counter

00:07:28.698 --> 00:07:31.159
and had pseudoephedrine, 
been less congested.

00:07:31.159 --> 00:07:34.328
But I didn't know there was anything wrong 
with phenylephrine.

00:07:34.829 --> 00:07:36.247
The advisory panel vote...

00:07:36.581 --> 00:07:38.749
that spreadsheet is a recommendation

00:07:38.749 --> 00:07:42.128
that the FDA still has to act on 
and implement.

00:07:42.587 --> 00:07:46.174
And the panelists voting, 
they seemed a little mystified

00:07:46.174 --> 00:07:48.885
by how we got to a point where 
phenylephrine took over.

00:07:49.093 --> 00:07:52.430
It's amazing the amount of dollars 
being spent on something

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:54.849
that has really no efficacy.

00:07:54.891 --> 00:07:57.101
I went through pharmacy school 
and medical school

00:07:57.101 --> 00:08:01.439
and I have taken courses on 
over-the-counter medications...

00:08:01.856 --> 00:08:04.984
and I had no idea until prepping for this

00:08:04.984 --> 00:08:06.903
how limited the data was.

00:08:07.069 --> 00:08:09.572
The drug companies have made billions of dollars...

00:08:09.947 --> 00:08:12.074
off of this medication.

00:08:12.158 --> 00:08:13.743
You know, for all these years.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.412
You know, people really don't like change.

00:08:16.913 --> 00:08:18.915
And this is going to change.

00:08:18.915 --> 00:08:22.335
You know, how the oh, according cost aisle

00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:25.796
look in the you know, 
in the pharmacy.

00:08:25.838 --> 00:08:28.758
That does not suggest in any way...

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:30.968
that they should not stay the course.

00:08:36.766 --> 00:08:38.684
I miraculously healed.

00:08:39.143 --> 00:08:40.228
Okay.

00:08:40.228 --> 00:08:41.187
My name is Phil Edwards.

00:08:41.187 --> 00:08:42.647
I'm the producer of this episode.

00:08:43.272 --> 00:08:45.274
I'm doing culture coverage.

00:08:45.316 --> 00:08:47.860
You've seen the awesome looking 
slate at the beginning.

00:08:48.069 --> 00:08:49.529
We're defining culture really broadly.

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:51.948
We're going to be talking about 
all sorts of different things...

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:54.492
that kind of effect the way 
that we live.

00:08:55.701 --> 00:08:58.120
I have one quick request for you before we go.

00:08:58.120 --> 00:08:58.955
Here at Vox...

00:08:59.247 --> 00:09:01.332
you know, our mission is to have...

00:09:01.666 --> 00:09:03.543
our journalism be free to everybody.

00:09:03.584 --> 00:09:05.628
We don't want to put it behind the counter.

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:08.172
That's a little callback to the video for you.

00:09:09.465 --> 00:09:10.508
We don't want to have a paywall.

00:09:10.508 --> 00:09:11.717
We don't want to do it.

00:09:12.009 --> 00:09:15.388
It's just not a value that the company has

00:09:15.388 --> 00:09:17.807
and that we have as part of the larger companies.

00:09:17.807 --> 00:09:20.101
Sometimes our work is supported 
by advertising

00:09:20.101 --> 00:09:21.352
but it's not enough.

00:09:21.352 --> 00:09:24.188
We need a little extra push to get us 
over the hump.

00:09:24.522 --> 00:09:26.232
And so that's why we're looking to you.

00:09:26.649 --> 00:09:29.569
If you can help keep our journalism free and support...

00:09:29.735 --> 00:09:33.155
our more ambitious work, 
go to www.vox.com/give-now

00:09:33.155 --> 00:09:34.323
to make a contribution.

00:09:34.365 --> 00:09:37.368
Your contributions support 
all our coverage across Vox

00:09:37.368 --> 00:09:38.744
and as a thank you to those of you

00:09:38.744 --> 00:09:40.955
who make recurring contributions

00:09:40.955 --> 00:09:45.209
we will give you access to newsletters, 
behind-the-scenes, Q&amp;A

00:09:45.209 --> 00:09:48.129
with journalists from across 
the whole Vox newsroom.

00:09:48.546 --> 00:09:49.171
All right.

00:09:49.171 --> 00:09:50.047
Thanks for watching.

00:09:50.381 --> 00:09:50.965
Bye.

